## Dialogue 2
It has been three months since I've been learning Korean.

**Daniel:** Nora, since when have you been learning Korean?

**Nora:** It has been three months since I've been learning Korean.

**Daniel:** Why are you studying Korean?

**Nora:** I am diligently studying Korean so I can go to graduate school.

**Daniel:** What do you want to study as your major in graduate school?

**Nora:** I will major in journalism and mass communication. I want to work at a broadcasting station after I graduate.

### Dialogue Vocabulary
- **대학원** - graduate school
- **열심히** - diligently
- **전공하다** - to major in
- **신문방송학** - journalism and mass communication
- **졸업하다** - to graduate

### Vocabulary Expansion
- **경영학** - business administration
- **경제학** - economics
- **교육학** - educational studies
- **국어국문학** - Korean language and literature
- **법학** - law
- **의학** - medical science
- **컴퓨터공학** - computer engineering
- **영문학** - English literature
- **새** - new

## Grammar

### 1. -은/ㄴ 지

**Meaning and Usage**
This is added to an action verb to show how much time has passed since a certain action has taken place.

**Form**
- **-ㄴ 지** is added when the verb stem ends in a vowel or 'ㄹ.'
- **-은 지** is added when the verb stem ends in a consonant.

| Action verb stem ending | Combine with | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Consonant | -은 지 | 먹은 지, 읽은 지 |
| vowel or ㄹ | -ㄴ 지 | 만난 지, 만든 지 |

**Example**
- 저는 대학교를 졸업한 지 삼 년 됐어요. - It has been three years since I graduated from university.
- 제 친구는 미국에 간 지 육 개월 됐어요. - It has been six months since my friend left for America.
- 저는 그 이야기를 들은 지 일주일 됐어요. - It has been one week since I heard that story.
- 리타오 씨, 기숙사에 산 지 얼마나 됐어요? - Letao, how long has it been since you have lived in the dorms?

**가:** 올리비아 씨, 태권도를 배운지 얼마나 됐어요? - Olivia, how long has it been since you have been learning Taekwondo?
**나:** 태권도를 배운 지 일 년 됐어요. - It has been one year since I have been learning Taekwondo.

**가:** 이 회사에서 언제부터 일했어요? - Since when have you started working in this company?
**나:** 이 회사에서 일한 지 삼 개월 됐어요. - It has been three months since I've been working in this company.

**Note:** This is used only with action verbs and cannot be used with descriptive verbs. Because the meaning of this form is already related to the past, it cannot be combined with '-었-.'

### 2. -으려고/려고

**Meaning and Usage**
This is added to an action verb to show the intention or purpose of the action stated in the sentence.

**Form**
- **-려고** is added when the verb stem ends with a vowel or 'ㄹ.'
- **-으려고** is added when the verb stem ends with a consonant.

| Action verb stem ending | Combine with | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Consonant | -으려고 | 먹으려고, 읽으려고 |
| vowel or ㄹ | -려고 | 만나려고, 만들려고 |

**Example**
- 에릭 씨가 친구를 만나려고 커피숍에 가요. - Eric is going to the coffee shop to meet his friend.
- 윤아 씨, 누구와 같이 먹으려고 김밥을 사요? - Yuna, with whom are you going to eat the gimbap you are buying?
- 저는 어머니께 드리려고 선물을 샀어요. - I bought a gift to give to my mom.
- 레오 씨는 불고기를 만들려고 소고기를 샀어요. - Leo bought some beef to make bulgogi.

**가:** 존 씨, 왜 잡지를 샀어요? - John, why did you buy the magazine?
**나:** 비행기에서 읽으려고 잡지를 샀어요. - I bought the magazine to read it on the airplane.

**가:** 하늘 씨, 무엇을 하려고 도서관에 갔어요? - Haneul, what brought you to the library?
**나:** 책을 빌리려고 도서관에 갔어요. - I went to the library to borrow a book.

**Note:** This is used with an action verb and not a descriptive verb. The subject for both clauses in the sentence must be the same, and for the most part, the subject is not mentioned in the second clause. This is not combined with the past tense '-었-' or the future form '-겠-.' An interrogative or imperative clause cannot follow at the end of the sentence.

