# Unit 8: Invitations

## Dialogue 1
We're thinking about starting the class meeting at around 7 o'clock.

**Leo:** I'm planning to have a class meeting at my house after the exam. Will you be able to come?

**Yuki:** Yes, I can be there. Will you be inviting everyone in class?

**Leo:** Yes. My wife will be making some of her best Korean food and will also prepare some of our hometown food.

**Yuki:** Wow! I'm looking forward to it. What time will the meeting start?

**Leo:** I'm thinking about starting it at around 7 o'clock.

**Yuki:** Please let me know if you need help. I can go a little early to help you.

### Dialogue Vocabulary
- **모임** - meeting or gathering
- **초대하다** - to invite
- **기대** - anticipation or expectation
- **시작하다** - to start
- **필요하다** - to need

### Vocabulary Expansion
- **강아지** - puppy
- **동창** - schoolmate
- **도움** - help
- **동아리** - school club
- **동창회** - alumni association
- **동호회** - workplace club
- **뒤풀이** - afterparty
- **송별회** - farewell party
- **초대장** - invitation card
- **환영회** - welcoming reception
- **회비** - (membership) fee
- **들다** - to join
- **모으다** - to gather
- **모이다** - to get together

## Grammar

### 1. –어/아/여, 이야/야

**Meaning and Usage**
This is used to show intimacy or familiarity in informal speech when you omit the '요' in the grammar form '-어요/아요/여요.' Informal speech is used when speaking to one's friends or juniors (subordinates) and even very close seniors. The form is the same for statements, questions, commands, and suggestions and is distinguished by intonation. The informal form of '이에요/예요' is '이야/야' and is used after a noun. The form is the same for statements and questions.

**Form**
- **'-아'** is used when the action or descriptive verb stem ends with 'ㅏ, ㅗ.'
- **'-어'** is used when it ends with the other vowels 'ㅓ,ㅜ,ㅡ,ㅣ'
- **'-여'(해)** is used when it ends with '하-.'
- **'이야'** is used when the noun ends with a consonant
- **'야'** is used when it ends with a vowel.

| Action or descriptive verb stem ending | Combine with | Example |
|---|---|---|
| ㅏ,ㅗ | -아 | 봐, 작아 |
| Other vowels (ㅓ,ㅜ,ㅡ,ㅣ) | -어 | 먹어, 입어, 커 |
| 하 | -여(해) | 좋아해, 깨끗해 |

| Noun ending syllable | Combine with | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Consonant | 이야 | 학생이야, 책이야 |
| Vowel | 야 | 친구야, 모자야 |

**Example**
- 나는 한국어 공부가 아주 재미있어. - I really enjoy studying Korean.
- 너는 오늘 어디에 가? - Where are you going today?
- 배가 많이 고프면 네가 먼저 먹어. - If you're really hungry, go ahead and eat first.
- 내가 제일 좋아하는 운동은 야구야. - My favorite sport is baseball.

**가:** 지난 주말에 뭘 했어? - What did you do last weekend?
**나:** 신촌에서 친구를 만났어. - I met my friend in Sinchon.

**가:** 오늘이 무슨 요일이야? - What day is today?
**나:** 목요일이야. - It's Thursday.

### 2. -을까/ㄹ까 하다

**Meaning and Usage**
This is used after an action verb to show the speaker's intention or desire for a certain action that has not yet been fully decided.

**Form**
- **'-ㄹ까 하다'** is used when the action verb stem ends with a vowel or 'ㄹ'
- **'-을까 하다'** is used when it ends with a consonant.

| Action verb stem ending | Combine with | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Consonant | -을까 하다 | 먹을까 하다, 읽을까 하다 |
| vowel, ㄹ | -ㄹ까 하다 | 만날까 하다, 만들까 하다 |

**Example**
- 이번 방학에 책을 많이 읽을까 해요. - I'm thinking about reading a lot of books during the vacation.
- 이번 주말에 나는 집에서 쉴까 해. - I'm considering relaxing at home this weekend.
- 운동을 시작해 볼까 하는데 어떤 운동이 좋을까? - I'm thinking about starting to exercise but what kind of exercise do you think is good?
- 저는 내년에 부모님을 모시고 여행을 갈까 합니다. - I'm thinking about taking my parents on a trip next year.

**가:** 유키 씨, 점심에 뭘 먹을 거예요? - Yuki, what will you eat for lunch?
**나:** 시간이 없어서 저는 김밥을 먹을까 해요. - I don't have time so I'm thinking about eating gimbap.

**가:** 너는 몇 급까지 공부할 거야? - Up to what level will you study?
**나:** 난 6급까지 공부할까 해. - I'm thinking about studying up to level six.

**Note:** This is used only with action verbs and cannot be used with descriptive verbs.

