**Haneul:** I see. It's been such a long time that I was hoping to see you. I'm sad you won't be able to come.

**Senior:** I'll go to next month's meeting. Make sure you give me a call.

**Haneul:** Yes, I'll definitely contact you.

### Dialogue Vocabulary
- **이틀** - two days
- **급하다** - to be urgent
- **후** - after
- **생기다** - to come into being
- **갑자기** - suddenly or unexpectedly
- **뵙다** - to meet (honorific form)
- **섭섭하다** - to be sad or disappointed

### Vocabulary Expansion
- **나흘** - four days
- **마흔** - forty
- **서른** - thirty
- **쉰** - fifty
- **여든** - eighty
- **닷새** - five days
- **사흘** - three days
- **_세** - age or years
- **아흔** - ninety
- **열흘** - ten days
- **이틀** - two days
- **예순** - sixty
- **일흔** - seventy
- **하루** - one day
- **세다** - to count

## Grammar

### 1. -어라/아라/여라, -자

**Meaning and Usage**
The informal form of '-으십시오/십시오' is '-어라/아라/여라.' This can be used with friends or one's subordinates. For the informal form for '-읍시다/ㅂ시다,' '-자' is used after the action verb stem.

**Form**
- **'-아라'** is used when the action verb stem ends with 'ㅏ, ㅗ.'
- **'-어라'** is used when the action verb stem ends with the other vowels 'ㅓ,ㅜ,ㅡ,ㅣ'
- **'-여라'(해라)** is used when it ends with '하-.'

| Action verb stem ending | Combine with | Example |
|---|---|---|
| ㅏ, ㅗ | -아라 | 사라, 봐라 |
| Other vowels (ㅓ,ㅜ,ㅡ,ㅣ) | -어라 | 먹어라, 만들어라 |
| 하 | -여라(해라) | 공부해라 |

**Example**
- 비가 오니까 우산을 가지고 가라. - Go and take an umbrella with you because it's raining.
- 하늘아, 약속 시간을 잊지 마라. - Haneul, don't forget the appointed time.
- 우리 만나서 커피 한 잔 하자. - Let's meet and have some coffee.
- 윤아야, 수업시간이니까 떠들지 말자. - Yuna, let's be quiet because it's class time.

**가:** 많이 먹어라. - Please eat a lot of food.
**나:** 네, 잘 먹겠습니다. - Yes, I will enjoy the meal.

**가:** 여기에서 사진을 찍을까? - Shall we take a picture here?
**나:** 그래, 여기에서 찍자. - Okay, let's take it here.

### 2. -게 되다

**Meaning and Usage**
This is used with an action verb stem and shows that something took place as a result of an external reason unrelated to the subject's will or wishes.

**Example**
- 에릭 씨가 일 때문에 외국에 가게 됐다. - Eric went to a foreign country because of work.
- 급한 일이 생겨서 약속을 지키지 못하게 됐어. - I wasn't able to keep my promise because an urgent matter arose.
- 저는 한국에 살아서 매운 음식을 잘 먹게 됐어요. - I was able to eat spicy food because I live in Korea.
- 지금 사는 집이 학교에서 멀어서 이사를 가게 됐습니다. - I had to move because the house I lived in was far from school.

**가:** 이번 주에 고향에 가요? - Are you going to your hometown this week?
**나:** 네, 일이 생겨서 고향에 돌아가게 됐어요. - Yes, something came up and so I have to go to my hometown.

**가:** 흐엉 씨, 어떻게 한국말을 공부하게 됐어요? - Huang, how did you start studying Korean?
**나:** 저는 한국 회사에 들어가고 싶어서 한국말을 배우게 됐어요. - I wanted to enter into a Korean company so I learned Korean.

**Note:** Although this is used for things that do not reflect the subject's active intention, in times when the result does reflect the subject's active intention, it can be used as an expression of humility as if it was not.

