## Dialogue 2
Excuse me but is Mr. Kim here?

**Leo:** Excuse me, but is Mr. Kim here?

**Employee:** He is not in the office right now. May I help you?

**Leo:** I came here because I wanted to see Mr. Kim. Do you know when he will return?

**Employee:** He has already left for the day. I think you will have to come back again tomorrow.

**Leo:** Then may I ask you to deliver this document to Mr. Kim?

**Employee:** Alright, I'll make sure I give it to him.

### Dialogue Vocabulary
- **저** - excuse me
- **이미** - already
- **퇴근하다** - to leave one's office or get off from work
- **서류** - document
- **전하다** - to deliver or hand over

### Vocabulary Expansion
- **부장** - head of a department or director
- **아** - ah
- **어** - uh
- **음** - um
- **저기** - excuse me
- **자** - okay

## Grammar

### 1. -습니다만/ㅂ니다만

**Meaning and Usage**
This is used with an action or descriptive verb to express something different from what was expected.

**Form**
- **'-ㅂ니다만'** is used when the action or descriptive verb stem ends with a vowel or 'ㄹ'
- **'-습니다만'** is used when it ends with a consonant.

| Action or descriptive verb stem ending | Combine with | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Consonant | -습니다만 | 먹습니다만, 작습니다만 |
| Vowel, ㄹ | -ㅂ니다만 | 좋아합니다만, 멉니다만 |

**Example**
- 음악을 좋아합니다만 음악회에는 자주 못 갑니다. - Although I like music, I'm not able to attend music concerts often.
- 죄송합니다만 잠깐 기다려 주십시오. - I'm sorry but please wait for a few minutes.
- 저는 한국 사람입니다만 한국말을 잘하지 못합니다. - I am Korean but I cannot speak Korean well.
- 한국생활에 많이 익숙해졌습니다만 아직 힘들 때가 있습니다. - Although I'm quite adjusted to life in Korea, there are still times when it's hard for me.

**가:** 실례합니다만 길 좀 물어봐도 될까요? - Excuse me, but may I ask for directions?
**나:** 네, 어디에 가시는데요? - Sure, where are you going?

**가:** 내일 저희 집 집들이에 오시겠습니까? - Can you attend the housewarming party at my house tomorrow?
**나:** 가고 싶습니다만 약속이 있어서 가기 어려울 것 같습니다. - I do want to go but it will be difficult for me to attend because I have another appointment.

**Note:** This is a more formal expression than '-지만' so it is used when respect is required or in formal situations.

### 2. -는지/은지/ㄴ지 알다(모르다)

**Meaning and Usage**
This is used with an action or descriptive verb to show when one knows or doesn't know about the content earlier in the sentence.

**Form**
- **'-는지 알다/모르다'** is used with all action verb stems including '있다' and '없다.'
- **'-ㄴ지 알다/모르다'** is used when a descriptive verb stem ends with a vowel or 'ㄹ'
- **'-은지 알다/모르다'** is used when it ends with a consonant.

| Verb stem ending | Combine with | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Action verb, 있다, 없다 | -는지 알다/모르다 | 가는지 알다/모르다, 있는지 알다/모르다 |
| Consonant (descriptive verb) | -은지 알다/모르다 | 넓은지 알다/모르다, 좋은지 알다/모르다 |
| vowel, ㄹ (descriptive verb) | -ㄴ지 알다/모르다 | 비싼지 알다/모르다, 먼지 알다/모르다 |

**Example**
- 강남역에 어떻게 가는지 아세요? - Do you know how to go to Gangnam Station?
- 오늘 학교에 왜 사람이 많은지 알아요? - Do you know why so many people are at school today?
- 저 사람이 누구인지 알아요. 에릭 씨 친구예요. - I know who that person is. He is Eric's friend.
- 두 사람이 어디에서 만났는지 몰라요. - I don't know where the two people have met.

**가:** 회의가 몇 시에 끝나는지 아세요? - Do you know when the meeting will be over?
**나:** 네, 알아요. 네 시쯤 끝날 거예요. - Yes, I do. It will end at around 4 o'clock.

**가:** 유키 씨 생일이 언제인지 알아요? - Do you know when Yuki's birthday is?
**나:** 글쎄요, 잘 모르겠어요. - Well, I'm not sure.

**Note:** Expressions related to questions that ask who(누구), where(어디), when(언제), how much(얼마), how(어떻게), or why(왜) precede the form '-는지/은지/ㄴ지 알다(모르다).'있다, 없다 | -나요? | 먹나요?, 가나요?, 있나요? |
| Consonant (descriptive verb) | -은가요? | 작은가요?, 좋은가요?, 싫은가요? |
| Vowel, ㄹ (descriptive verb) | -ㄴ가요? | 큰가요?, 친절한가요?, 먼가요? |

**Example**
- 리타오 씨는 몇 시에 집에 오나요? - What time do you come home, Letao?
- 김치찌개가 매운가요? - Is the kimchi-jjigae (kimchi stew) spicy?
- 올리비아 씨 친구는 어느 나라 사람인가요? - What country is Olivia's friend from?
- 에릭 씨, 방 청소를 다 했나요? - Eric, are you finished with cleaning your room?

**가:** 리타오 씨, 어느 나라에서 오셨나요? - Letao, what country did you come from?
**나:** 저는 중국에서 왔어요. - I came from China.

**가:** 유키 씨, 친구 생일이 언제인가요? - Yuki, when is your friend's birthday?
**나:** 다음 주 토요일이에요. - It is next Saturday.

**Note:** When the descriptive verbs '이다' and '아니다' are combined with the past tense '-었-,' you use '-었나요.'

